VALUES

-- Q1: easy / L1 — Preferred redemption premium in basic EPS numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, when preferred stock is redeemed at an amount exceeding its carrying value, how is the excess treated in the basic EPS calculation?', '["The excess is added to net income in the numerator", "The excess is deducted from net income in the numerator", "The excess is ignored because it is a balance sheet transaction", "The excess is disclosed in the footnotes only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-S99-2, when preferred stock is redeemed at an amount exceeding its carrying value, the excess (redemption premium) is treated as a return to preferred shareholders and deducted from net income in computing the basic EPS numerator. This reduces income available to common shareholders. Unlike choice A, the premium is never added — it always reduces earnings available to common. Choice C is incorrect because the premium directly affects EPS despite being a balance sheet event.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: easy / L2 — DRIP shares and weighted-average calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Foxworth Corp. has 300,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On October 1, 2025, shareholders in the dividend reinvestment plan (DRIP) receive 6,000 newly issued shares in lieu of a cash dividend. What is the weighted-average number of common shares for 2025?', '["306,000", "301,500", "300,000", "304,500"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, shares issued through a DRIP are new shares issued for consideration (the reinvested dividend) and are weighted from the date of issuance. The 6,000 shares are outstanding for 3 out of 12 months: 300,000 + (6,000 x 3/12) = 301,500. Unlike a stock dividend, DRIP shares are not applied retroactively because shareholders pay fair value for them. Choice A (306,000) incorrectly treats the DRIP shares as outstanding for the entire year.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Anti-dilution sequencing: security becomes antidilutive mid-sequence
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Harwick Corp. has basic EPS of $3.00 (net income $600,000, 200,000 shares). Three potentially dilutive securities are ranked: Security X adds 5,000 incremental shares ($0 numerator impact), Security Y adds $6,000 after-tax interest and 4,000 shares, and Security Z adds $12,000 in preferred dividends and 3,000 shares. After including X, EPS is $2.93. After including Y, EPS is $2.90. Evaluate whether Security Z should be included.', '["Security Z is excluded because its per-share impact of $4.00 exceeds the cumulative diluted EPS of $2.90", "Security Z is included because all convertible securities must be tested", "Security Z is excluded only if it is antidilutive relative to basic EPS of $3.00", "Security Z is included because it reduces EPS below $2.90"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, securities are tested sequentially from most to least dilutive. Security Z''s per-share impact is $12,000/3,000 = $4.00. Because $4.00 exceeds the running diluted EPS of $2.90, including Z would increase EPS (antidilutive). Once a security is antidilutive in sequence, it and all remaining securities are excluded. Choice C is incorrect because the test compares to the running diluted EPS, not to basic EPS.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Induced conversion expense does not reduce EPS numerator separately
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, when a company pays an inducement to encourage early conversion of convertible bonds, how does the inducement expense affect the basic EPS numerator?', '["The inducement is added back to net income in the numerator", "The inducement has no separate EPS adjustment because it already reduced net income", "The inducement is deducted from net income similarly to preferred dividends", "The inducement is excluded from both the numerator and denominator"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 470-20-40-16, the inducement expense is recognized in the income statement, reducing net income. For basic EPS purposes under ASC 260-10-45-5, no separate adjustment is needed because net income already reflects the charge. Choice C is incorrect because the inducement is not analogous to a preferred dividend — it is an expense already embedded in net income and requires no further deduction.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Pre-exercise period: if-converted treatment for bonds actually converted mid-year
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On January 1, 2025, bondholders of Trellis Manufacturing held $800,000 face value, 6% debentures convertible into 20,000 equity shares. All bondholders exercised the conversion privilege on April 1. Trellis reports annual net income of $700,000 and applies a 25% tax rate. The CFO excluded the debentures from the diluted EPS computation because they no longer exist at year-end. Assess whether this exclusion is appropriate.', '["The exclusion is appropriate because only securities outstanding at year-end are tested for dilution", "The exclusion is inappropriate; the pre-conversion quarter requires the if-converted method, adding $9,000 after-tax interest and weighting 20,000 shares for 3 months", "The exclusion is inappropriate; the full annual interest of $36,000 after tax must be added regardless of conversion date", "The exclusion is appropriate because actual conversion eliminates the hypothetical assumption"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, when convertible securities are actually converted during the period, the if-converted method must be applied for the portion of the period before conversion. For January through March (3 months): after-tax interest = $800,000 x 6% x 3/12 x (1 - 0.25) = $9,000, and 20,000 shares are weighted for 3/12 = 5,000 incremental shares. Choice A is incorrect because diluted EPS considers all potentially dilutive events during the period, not just those existing at year-end.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — ESOP shares: committed-to-be-released vs suspense shares in EPS denominator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fairhaven Corp. sponsors a leveraged ESOP holding 80,000 shares in a suspense account. During 2025, 15,000 shares were committed to be released to participants based on debt service payments. The ESOP also holds 25,000 shares previously allocated to participant accounts. Fairhaven has 200,000 non-ESOP shares outstanding and net income of $720,000. Evaluate which ESOP shares should be included in the weighted-average denominator for basic EPS.', '["All 80,000 suspense shares plus the 25,000 allocated shares (105,000 total ESOP shares)", "Only the 25,000 allocated shares; suspense and committed-to-be-released shares are excluded", "Only the 15,000 committed-to-be-released shares plus the 25,000 allocated shares (40,000 total ESOP shares)", "Only the 80,000 suspense shares; allocated shares are excluded because participants may forfeit them"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 718-40-45-3, ESOP shares included in the weighted-average denominator for basic EPS are those allocated to participant accounts plus shares committed to be released. Unallocated suspense shares (80,000 - 15,000 = 65,000) are excluded because they have not yet been earned by participants. Total ESOP shares in denominator: 15,000 + 25,000 = 40,000. Choice B incorrectly excludes committed-to-be-released shares, which are substantively earned.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Who controls the settlement election determines diluted EPS presumption
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgepoint Corp. entered into a structured equity-linked obligation that the counterparty may redeem for either 25,000 newly issued Ridgepoint shares or $1,200,000 in cash — the election rests solely with the counterparty. Ridgepoint''s treasurer asserts the obligation should be excluded from diluted EPS because management intends to settle in cash and has the liquidity to do so. Assess the treasurer''s position under the settlement-election guidance.', '["The treasurer is correct; demonstrated liquidity and stated intent to pay cash override the counterparty''s option", "The treasurer is incorrect; when the counterparty holds the election, the more dilutive settlement alternative must be assumed regardless of management''s intent", "The treasurer is correct; only formal written notice from the counterparty triggers inclusion", "The treasurer is incorrect; all equity-linked obligations must use the reverse treasury stock method"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35 through 45-36, when the counterparty controls the settlement choice, the more dilutive of share settlement or cash settlement is assumed for diluted EPS purposes. Management''s intent and liquidity position are irrelevant because the counterparty, not the entity, decides. Choice A is incorrect — company intent governs only when the entity itself controls the settlement election.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Excess of preferred liquidation value over par as EPS deduction
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Grandview Corp. has 10,000 shares of preferred stock with a $50 par value and a $65 per share liquidation preference. The preferred stock is not mandatorily redeemable and was issued at par. For the current year, Grandview declares and pays the $4 per share annual dividend. Net income is $800,000 with 200,000 common shares. What amount is deducted from net income for the basic EPS numerator?', '["$190,000", "$40,000", "$65,000", "$150,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, only declared preferred dividends reduce the basic EPS numerator for noncumulative preferred, and the period''s dividend for cumulative preferred. The $4 per share dividend totals $40,000 (10,000 x $4). The liquidation preference premium ($15 per share excess over par) does not reduce the numerator unless the preferred stock is actually being redeemed. Choice A ($190,000) incorrectly adds the liquidation premium to the dividend deduction.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — EPS-based contingent shares and circularity
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pemberton Corp. agreed to issue 30,000 additional shares if annual EPS exceeds $4.00 in any year through 2027. In 2025, Pemberton has net income of $960,000 and 200,000 weighted-average shares, producing basic EPS of $4.80. However, if the 30,000 contingent shares are included in diluted EPS, the denominator increases to 230,000 shares and EPS falls to $4.17. Evaluate whether the contingent shares should be included in diluted EPS.', '["Exclude the shares because their inclusion reduces EPS, creating a circular calculation", "Include only a pro-rata portion (enough to bring EPS to exactly $4.00)", "Include the 30,000 shares because, even after inclusion, EPS still exceeds the $4.00 threshold", "Exclude the shares because the condition has not been met for three consecutive years"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-52, contingently issuable shares tied to an EPS target are included in diluted EPS if the target would still be met after the contingent shares are added to the denominator. Here, including 30,000 shares produces EPS of $4.17, which still exceeds $4.00. The condition remains satisfied after inclusion, so all 30,000 shares are added. Choice A is incorrect — the circularity concern is resolved by the post-inclusion test, not by excluding the shares entirely.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Loss allocation asymmetry: participating preferred does not absorb losses
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thorndale Corp. has 200,000 common shares and 50,000 shares of participating preferred stock. In 2025, Thorndale reports a net loss of $600,000 with no dividends declared. Under the two-class method, the controller allocates $120,000 of the loss to the participating preferred (50,000/250,000 x $600,000). Evaluate this allocation.', '["The allocation is correct because participating preferred shares in all earnings, including losses", "The allocation is incorrect; losses are split equally regardless of share counts", "The allocation is incorrect; restricted shares are always excluded from EPS entirely", "The allocation is incorrect; losses are not allocated to participating preferred unless it is contractually obligated to fund losses"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-66, losses are allocated to participating securities under the two-class method only if the security has a contractual obligation to share in the entity''s losses. If the preferred has no obligation to fund losses, the entire $600,000 loss is allocated to common shareholders. Choice A is incorrect because participation in earnings does not automatically mean participation in losses — the obligation must be contractual.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — Interest add-back using effective rate (not coupon) for bonds at discount
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ashland Corp. issued $1,000,000 face value, 5% convertible bonds at 92, producing effective interest expense of $58,000 annually. Each $1,000 bond converts into 25 common shares. The tax rate is 25%. When computing diluted EPS, the controller adds back $37,500 (the after-tax coupon of $50,000 x 75%) to the numerator. Evaluate the error and determine the correct add-back.', '["The controller should add back $43,500 ($58,000 x 0.75) because the effective interest expense, not the coupon, is the correct amount", "The controller''s calculation of $37,500 is correct because only the coupon is added back", "The controller should add back $58,000 without a tax adjustment", "The controller should add back $50,000 pre-tax because tax effects are ignored for convertible bonds"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the interest expense added back in the if-converted numerator adjustment is the actual interest expense recognized in the income statement — the effective interest, not the stated coupon. For bonds issued at a discount, effective interest ($58,000) exceeds coupon interest ($50,000). After tax: $58,000 x 0.75 = $43,500. Choice B understates the add-back by using the coupon rate, which ignores the discount amortization already charged to income.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: hard / L3 — Share lending arrangement exclusion from EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Whitmore Corp. lends 50,000 of its own shares to an investment bank under a share lending arrangement in connection with the bank''s convertible bond offering for Whitmore. The shares must be returned to Whitmore upon bond maturity or conversion. The controller includes the 50,000 lent shares in the weighted-average denominator because they are legally outstanding. Evaluate whether this treatment is correct.', '["The treatment is correct because legally outstanding shares are always included in the denominator", "The treatment is incorrect; the shares should be included only at half weight", "The treatment is correct because the shares will eventually be returned", "The treatment is incorrect; loaned shares issued for virtually no consideration in a share lending arrangement are excluded from the denominator"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-13A, shares issued in a share lending arrangement entered into in connection with a convertible debt offering are excluded from the basic and diluted EPS denominator when issued for little or no consideration and the borrower is contractually required to return the shares. They are not substantive issuances. Choice A is incorrect because legal form does not govern — the economic substance of the arrangement requires exclusion.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Written call options use treasury stock method (not reverse TSM)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Glendale Corp. writes call options allowing holders to purchase 15,000 shares at $30 per share. The average market price during 2025 is $50. Net income is $900,000 with 200,000 weighted-average shares. The CFO asks whether these written call options affect diluted EPS differently from employee stock options. Evaluate the treatment.', '["Written call options are treated the same as employee stock options under the treasury stock method, adding 6,000 incremental shares", "Written call options are excluded from diluted EPS because the entity received a premium", "Written call options use the reverse treasury stock method, not the treasury stock method", "Written call options add 15,000 shares to the denominator with no assumed repurchase"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, written call options (including warrants sold by the entity) use the treasury stock method just like employee stock options. The assumed proceeds ($30 x 15,000 = $450,000) repurchase $450,000 / $50 = 9,000 shares, yielding 15,000 - 9,000 = 6,000 incremental shares. Choice C is incorrect — the reverse treasury stock method applies to written put options, not written call options.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Two-class method: participation ceiling limits undistributed earnings allocation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Brentwood Inc. issued 60,000 preferred units with a $1.50 base distribution and a contractual ceiling of $3.00 total per unit on all distributions (base plus participation). After paying the full $3.00 per unit ($180,000) during 2025, Brentwood reports $1,200,000 of net income and $400,000 of distributions to its 300,000 ordinary units. Under the two-class allocation, how much of the remaining $620,000 undistributed profit is allocable to the preferred units?', '["$620,000 x 60,000 / 360,000 = $103,333", "Zero, because the $3.00 per-unit ceiling has already been reached through declared distributions", "The ceiling applies only in periods when all preferred dividends were paid", "The preferred units receive the same per-unit allocation as ordinary units with no ceiling adjustment"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, the two-class method must respect contractual caps on participation. The preferred units already received $3.00 per unit ($180,000 / 60,000), which equals the ceiling. No additional undistributed profit may be allocated to them — the entire $620,000 goes to ordinary units. Choice A incorrectly applies a proportional allocation that ignores the contractual ceiling entirely.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Equity method investee dilution reflected in parent diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, when a parent company holds a significant-influence equity method investment, how is the investee''s dilution reflected in the parent''s diluted EPS?', '["The investee''s dilutive securities are added directly to the parent''s denominator", "The parent uses diluted earnings from the investee (rather than basic) in its own diluted EPS numerator", "The investee''s dilution is ignored for the parent''s EPS computation", "The parent converts the investee''s securities as if they were its own"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-26, diluted EPS of the parent should include the dilutive effect of securities issued by subsidiaries, joint ventures, or equity method investees. For equity method investments, this is accomplished by using the investee''s diluted (not basic) earnings when computing the parent''s equity pickup for the numerator. Choice A is incorrect because the investee''s securities are not added to the parent''s denominator directly.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — Accelerated share repurchase (ASR) effect on weighted-average shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, when a company enters into an accelerated share repurchase (ASR) program and receives an initial delivery of shares on the trade date, how are those shares treated in computing weighted-average shares for basic EPS?', '["The shares are excluded until the ASR contract settles in full", "The initial shares received reduce the weighted-average from the delivery date", "The shares reduce the weighted-average from the announcement date, not the delivery date", "The shares are included in the denominator until retirement"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11A, shares delivered under an ASR program are treated as retired for EPS purposes on the date they are delivered. The initial delivery immediately reduces the weighted-average share count from that date forward. Choice A is incorrect because waiting for final settlement would delay the EPS impact — the accounting follows actual share delivery, not contract completion.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Deep in-the-money (penny) warrants produce near-full dilution
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, when outstanding warrants have an exercise price of $0.01 per share (penny warrants), what is the most likely result when applying the treasury stock method?', '["The warrants are excluded because the nominal exercise price signals they are not genuine equity instruments", "Nearly all warrant shares are added as incremental shares because the assumed repurchase from minimal proceeds is negligible", "Zero incremental shares result because assumed proceeds equal the fair value of shares issued", "The warrants are treated as contingently issuable shares rather than under the treasury stock method"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-23, the treasury stock method assumes warrant proceeds ($0.01 per share) are used to repurchase shares at the average market price. With near-zero proceeds, virtually no shares are repurchased, so nearly all warrant shares become incremental dilutive shares. For example, 10,000 penny warrants at $50 market price yield 10,000 - ($100/$50) = 9,998 incremental shares. Choice C is incorrect because proceeds are trivial, not equal to fair value.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Reverse acquisition: whose shares are used for EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Glenfield Inc. (legal acquirer, 100,000 shares outstanding) acquires Thornbury Corp. (legal acquiree, 400,000 shares outstanding) in a reverse acquisition where Thornbury is the accounting acquirer under ASC 805. For the period before the acquisition, Thornbury''s historical EPS must be restated. Analyze how the weighted-average shares for EPS are determined.', '["Both periods use Glenfield''s shares because Glenfield is the legal acquirer", "Pre-acquisition EPS uses Glenfield''s actual shares; post-acquisition uses combined shares", "Pre-acquisition EPS uses Thornbury''s historical shares without adjustment", "Pre-acquisition EPS uses Thornbury''s historical shares multiplied by the exchange ratio; post-acquisition uses actual combined shares"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-67, in a reverse acquisition, the accounting acquirer''s (Thornbury''s) historical weighted-average shares are retroactively adjusted by the exchange ratio to reflect the legal acquirer''s (Glenfield''s) capital structure. Post-acquisition, the actual outstanding shares of the combined entity are used. Choice C is incorrect because an exchange ratio adjustment is required to make historical EPS comparable to the post-acquisition share count.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Antidilutive securities footnote disclosure requirement
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260-10-50, what must a company disclose about securities that were excluded from the diluted EPS computation because they were antidilutive?', '["No disclosure is required for antidilutive securities", "A description of the securities and the reason they were excluded", "Only the total number of antidilutive shares across all securities", "Antidilutive securities must be reclassified and reported as contingent liabilities"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-50-1(c), entities must disclose securities (including options, warrants, and convertible instruments) that could potentially dilute basic EPS in the future but were excluded from diluted EPS because they were antidilutive. The disclosure includes a description of those securities. Choice A is incorrect because antidilutive securities still require footnote disclosure even though they are excluded from the computation itself.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — Net share settlement convertible notes: treasury stock method vs if-converted
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thorngate Corp. issues $2,000,000 of 3% convertible notes that require cash settlement of the principal and permit share settlement only for the conversion spread (the excess of conversion value over principal). The notes convert into a variable number of shares based on a $40 conversion price. During 2025, the average market price is $55 and net income is $1,800,000 with 500,000 weighted-average shares. The CFO applies the if-converted method, adding $45,000 after-tax interest and 50,000 conversion shares. Evaluate this approach.', '["The CFO''s approach is correct because all convertible instruments use the if-converted method", "The CFO should exclude the notes entirely because they settle principal in cash", "For the principal portion settled in cash, no share dilution arises; only the conversion spread uses the treasury stock method, not the if-converted method", "The CFO should use the reverse treasury stock method for net-share-settled instruments"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-44 (as amended by ASU 2020-06 for instruments issued after December 15, 2021), convertible instruments with a net-share settlement feature apply the if-converted method. However, for instruments issued before the ASU effective date where the cash conversion feature was bifurcated, only the conversion spread was dilutive using the treasury stock method. Choice A is incorrect because the treatment depends on whether ASU 2020-06 applies — legacy instruments used a different approach.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Interim period control number tested independently each quarter
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgemont Corp. reports Q1 income from continuing operations of $300,000 and Q2 loss from continuing operations of $(100,000). In Q1, stock options adding 4,000 incremental shares are dilutive. For the Q2 standalone interim report, the options would still add incremental shares, but Q2 has a loss from continuing operations. How should the options be treated in Q2 diluted EPS?', '["Include the options in Q2 diluted EPS because they were dilutive in Q1", "Include the options in Q2 because the year-to-date income is still positive", "Exclude the options from Q2 diluted EPS because the Q2 control number (loss from continuing operations) makes all potential shares antidilutive", "Include the options at a reduced weighting based on the Q2 loss magnitude"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19 and ASC 260-10-55-41, the control number for each interim period is that period''s income (or loss) from continuing operations. When Q2 has a loss from continuing operations, all potentially dilutive securities become antidilutive for Q2 and are excluded from Q2 diluted EPS. Choice A is incorrect because each period''s dilution test is independent — Q1 dilution does not carry over to Q2.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Contingently issuable shares based on a market-price condition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hargrove Holdings agreed to issue 20,000 additional shares to former owners of an acquired company if Hargrove''s stock price exceeds $60 for 30 consecutive trading days by December 31, 2026. At December 31, 2025, the stock price is $48 and has never exceeded $60. Hargrove has 300,000 weighted-average shares and net income of $900,000. Evaluate the 2025 EPS treatment of the contingent shares.', '["Include 20,000 shares in both basic and diluted EPS because the condition could still be met", "Include 20,000 shares in basic EPS only", "Exclude from basic EPS; include in diluted EPS only if the market-price condition is currently met at the reporting date", "Exclude from both basic and diluted EPS because the shares are not currently issuable"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in basic EPS only when the contingency condition is met as of the reporting date. For diluted EPS under ASC 260-10-45-52, market-price conditions are evaluated based on whether the condition is currently satisfied. Since the $60 threshold was never met, the shares are excluded from both basic and diluted EPS. Choice A is incorrect because potential future satisfaction does not warrant inclusion.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Unvested restricted shares with forfeitable dividends excluded from two-class
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Brookfield Corp. grants 20,000 shares of unvested restricted stock to employees. The award agreement specifies that dividends on unvested shares are forfeitable if the employee terminates before vesting. Net income is $800,000, total dividends declared are $200,000, and there are 180,000 common shares outstanding. The controller applies the two-class method, allocating a portion of undistributed earnings to the unvested restricted shares. Evaluate this approach.', '["The controller is correct because all restricted stock is a participating security", "The controller is incorrect; restricted shares are always excluded from EPS entirely", "The controller is incorrect; restricted shares with forfeitable dividends are not participating securities and the two-class method does not apply to them", "The controller is correct because vesting is probable"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-59A, unvested share-based payment awards with forfeitable dividend rights are not participating securities. The two-class method applies only when unvested shares have nonforfeitable dividend rights. Because Brookfield''s dividends are forfeitable upon termination, these restricted shares should instead be treated under the treasury stock method for diluted EPS. Choice A is incorrect — forfeitability of dividends is the key distinction for determining participating security status.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Assumed proceeds in treasury stock method include unamortized compensation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which amounts are included in the assumed proceeds when applying the treasury stock method to employee stock options for diluted EPS?', '["Only the exercise price", "The exercise price plus any unamortized compensation cost attributable to future services", "The exercise price minus any tax benefit", "The average market price of the stock"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, assumed proceeds include the option exercise price, the amount of compensation cost not yet recognized (attributable to future services), and any excess tax benefits that would be credited to additional paid-in capital. Choice A is incorrect because using only the exercise price understates assumed proceeds, which would overstate the dilutive effect by assuming fewer shares are repurchased.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Incremental shares from warrants: proceeds include premium received
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Edgemont Corp. sold 10,000 warrants to outside investors at $5 per warrant. Each warrant entitles the holder to purchase one share at $30. The average market price during 2025 is $50. Under the treasury stock method, what are the assumed proceeds per warrant for computing incremental shares?', '["$30 per warrant (exercise price only)", "$50 per warrant (average market price)", "$35 per warrant (exercise price plus premium received)", "$25 per warrant (exercise price minus premium)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, assumed proceeds under the treasury stock method include the exercise price plus the amount of compensation cost not yet recognized plus any premium or discount from the original issuance. For warrants sold at $5, total assumed proceeds = $30 + $5 = $35 per warrant. Shares repurchased = $350,000 / $50 = 7,000. Incremental shares = 10,000 - 7,000 = 3,000. Choice A ignores the premium, which would overstate the dilutive effect.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Stock split during interim: retroactive restatement of all prior periods
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hawthorne Inc. reported basic EPS of $3.60 for Year 1. In March of Year 2, Hawthorne executes a 3-for-1 stock split. The controller presents Year 1 comparative EPS as $3.60 (unchanged). Evaluate whether this presentation is correct.', '["The presentation is correct because the split occurred in Year 2", "The presentation is incorrect; Year 1 EPS should be $2.40", "The presentation is correct if the split is disclosed in a footnote", "The presentation is incorrect; Year 1 EPS must be retroactively restated to $1.20 to reflect the stock split"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, when a stock split occurs after the balance sheet date but before financial statement issuance, all prior-period EPS must be retroactively restated. Year 1 EPS becomes $3.60 / 3 = $1.20 to ensure comparability. Choice A is incorrect because the timing of the split in Year 2 does not exempt the entity from retroactive restatement — comparability requires adjustment of all prior periods presented.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Effect of treasury stock on basic EPS denominator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, how are treasury shares treated when computing the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding for basic EPS?', '["Treasury shares remain in the weighted-average until formally retired", "Treasury shares are included at half weight", "Treasury shares are excluded only if repurchased at above market price", "Treasury shares are excluded from the weighted-average from the date of repurchase"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding excludes treasury stock. When shares are repurchased, they are removed from the weighted-average calculation from the date of repurchase forward. Choice A is incorrect because legal retirement is not required — repurchase alone is sufficient to remove shares from the outstanding count for EPS purposes.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Year-to-date diluted EPS: options dilutive quarterly but antidilutive annually
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fieldstone Inc. has 10,000 stock options with an exercise price of $30. In Q1, the average market price was $45, producing 3,333 incremental shares. For the full year, the average market price was $28. The controller includes 3,333 incremental shares in annual diluted EPS because they were dilutive in Q1. Evaluate whether this approach is correct.', '["The approach is correct because once options are dilutive in any quarter, they must be included annually", "The approach is incorrect; the annual diluted EPS test uses the annual average market price of $28, making the options antidilutive for the year", "The approach is correct under a quarter-by-quarter aggregation method", "The approach is incorrect; options that are dilutive in any quarter are included for only that quarter''s weight"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, the dilutive effect of stock options for annual diluted EPS is determined using the annual average market price, not individual quarterly averages. Since the annual average price of $28 is below the $30 exercise price, the options are antidilutive for the annual period and are excluded. Choice A incorrectly assumes quarterly dilution persists into annual computations — each period is tested independently.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Noncumulative preferred: declared vs undeclared dividend treatment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Prescott Corp. has 8,000 shares of noncumulative preferred stock with a $5 annual dividend per share. In 2025, the board declares only $20,000 in preferred dividends (half the full amount). Net income is $500,000 with 150,000 common shares. What is basic EPS?', '["$3.07", "$3.33", "$2.93", "$3.20"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, for noncumulative preferred stock, only dividends actually declared are deducted from the numerator. The full $40,000 is not deducted — only the $20,000 declared amount. Basic EPS = ($500,000 - $20,000) / 150,000 = $3.20. Choice A ($3.07) incorrectly deducts the full $40,000 annual amount ($500,000 - $40,000 = $460,000 / 150,000 = $3.07), which is the treatment for cumulative preferred, not noncumulative.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Contingently convertible bonds (CoCo bonds) in diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Sentinel Corp. issues $5,000,000 of 4% contingently convertible (CoCo) bonds. The bonds convert into 100,000 common shares only if Sentinel''s stock price exceeds $80 for 20 consecutive trading days. During 2025, the stock price never reached $80 (average price $55). The tax rate is 25%. Sentinel has net income of $3,000,000 and 600,000 common shares. The controller excludes the bonds from diluted EPS because the market trigger was never met. Evaluate whether this is correct.', '["The controller is correct; the bonds are only included once the trigger condition is satisfied", "The controller is incorrect; under ASC 260-10-45-42, contingently convertible bonds must be included in diluted EPS under the if-converted method regardless of whether the market price trigger has been met", "The controller is incorrect; the bonds should be included under the treasury stock method", "The controller is correct because market-price contingencies are treated differently from earnings contingencies"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-42 and EITF 04-8, contingently convertible instruments are included in diluted EPS using the if-converted method regardless of whether the contingent trigger has been met, as long as the effect is dilutive. The after-tax interest add-back is $5,000,000 x 4% x 0.75 = $150,000, and 100,000 shares are added. Choice A is incorrect — it contradicts the requirement to assume conversion for diluted EPS purposes even when the market price condition is unmet.', 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
